{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-130339",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}+3B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3A+3B=0.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=2 \\\\ -3A+3B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1,B=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+{e}^{3x}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
